.TH "SKARN-RESTORE" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.28.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-restore \- Restore a session
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBrestore\fR \fIsession\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print the shell command that resumes a session, so `eval $(skarn restore
<session>)` returns you to its working directory. When the recorded working
directory or session id carries a byte the running shell would read back as
something else, skarn refuses to render the command: stdout stays empty, the
refusal is named on stderr, and restore exits 1.
.PP
Restore a session by id. The resume command is printed from the raw session,
because a masked working directory would not be a usable command: when the
recorded working directory carries a detected secret, restore refuses with
exit 1 and prints nothing to stdout, and \-\-no\-redact prints it as recorded.
The match hint on stderr masks the project, branch and assistant name.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-redact\fR
Print raw session content and skip the detection scan. By default every
detected secret is masked in the output; the match and every filter run on the
raw text either way, so a masked value still finds its session.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Session id to operate on (prefix\-matched); equivalent to the positional
argument.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
